  "worked_solution": "First, we find ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ and its magnitude $\u2016{r}^{\u2032}(t)\u2016:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t-2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\u27e9 \\\\ \u2016{r}^{\u2032}(t)\u2016 & =\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)}^{2}+{(-3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}\u2061t+9{cos}^{2}\u2061t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}\u2061t+{cos}^{2}\u2061t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\u2016{r}^{\u2032}(u)\u2016\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3u\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $s=3t,$ we have that $t=\\frac{s}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{3}) \\\\  & =\u27e83\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{s}{3})-2,\\,1-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{s}{3})\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
